Knowing **how to digitize hotel SOPs** well comes down to one decision: whether you are moving documents or moving work. Scanning the binder into a shared drive moves documents. Turning each standard into a timed task on the phone of the person doing the job moves work, and only that version changes what happens on the floor.

This article walks through one real conversion, end to end. The standard is a guest room reset, the most common SOP in any hotel. By the end you will have a template you can apply to the rest of your library. For the full framework, including what to convert first and how to measure it, see our [guide to digitizing hotel SOPs](/digital-sop).

## How to digitize hotel SOPs, starting from a binder page

Here is a typical paper version, lightly anonymised from the kind of document most properties hold:

> **HSK-101 Guest Room Reset.** Upon entering the room, the attendant should ensure the room is ventilated and all used linen is removed. The bathroom must be thoroughly cleaned and sanitised, with particular attention to the vanity, mirror and fittings, which should be free of water marks. Amenities are to be replenished as per the standard par. The bed must be made according to brand standard with hospital corners and the decorative runner centred. All surfaces should be dusted. Before leaving, the attendant must check the room from the guest's perspective and report any maintenance issues to the supervisor.

It is a good document. It is also nearly impossible to execute consistently, for three reasons. It is written as prose, so the steps are buried. It has no time expectation, so nobody can tell whether a room took too long or too little. And it produces no evidence: the only proof that it ran is a supervisor's walk-past.

## Step 1: Pull out the actions

Read the prose and list every action as a single line. The rule is simple: **one step, one action, one thing a person can confirm is done.**

1. Ventilate the room.
2. Strip bed and remove used linen.
3. Clean and sanitise bathroom.
4. Polish vanity, mirror and fittings.
5. Restock amenities to par.
6. Make bed with hospital corners.
7. Centre the decorative runner.
8. Dust and polish surfaces.
9. Guest-eye scan.
10. Report maintenance issues.

Notice what happened to "thoroughly" and "particular attention". Adjectives do not survive the conversion. Either they become a separate, checkable step (step 4 above), or they go.

## Step 2: Group the steps into phases

Ten steps in a flat list are still hard to follow on a small screen. Grouping them into phases gives the attendant a sense of where they are. Mise uses four phases for most standards:

| Phase | Steps |
|---|---|
| Prepare | Ventilate · strip bed and remove linen |
| Perform | Clean bathroom · polish fittings · restock amenities · make bed · centre runner · dust surfaces |
| Verify | Guest-eye scan · report maintenance issues |
| Release | Room released to front office |

The Release phase is new. The paper version never said when the room was finished. The digital version has to, because release is the moment the front desk can use the room, and the moment the task writes to the [service record](/glossary/service-record).

## Step 3: Set a target time you can defend

A target time turns a standard into something you can plan and measure. The mistake is to set an aspirational number. Instead, time three or four competent attendants doing the standard as written, take a figure close to the median, and round it. If your competent people take around 26 minutes, the target is 26 minutes, not 20.

You will revise it. In the first weeks, the service record will show you which rooms run consistently over and why: a floor with older bathrooms, a room type with extra furniture, a step that is genuinely slower than anyone thought. That is useful information, not failure.

## Step 4: Add reference photos

Most arguments about standards are arguments about what "done" looks like. A photo settles them. For HSK-101 we attach three reference photos:

- the finished bathroom vanity from the doorway;
- the made bed with the runner centred;
- the amenity tray at par.

Keep reference photos simple, well lit and taken from the angle the attendant will see. They are not marketing images; they are the standard, made visible.

## Step 5: Choose the photo gates

A [photo gate](/glossary/photo-gate) is a step that cannot be ticked until a photo is taken from inside the task. It is the single most powerful feature of a digital SOP, and the easiest to overuse.

For HSK-101 we gate two steps: **polish vanity, mirror and fittings** (the detail guests photograph and complain about) and **guest-eye scan** (the final check, captured as a wide shot). That is enough evidence for a supervisor to review the room remotely and for an auditor to see the standard ran.

Gating all ten steps would slow the attendant, fill the record with meaningless images and train people to take photos without looking. The rule we use: gate the steps a guest or an auditor would check.

## Step 6: Publish into the shift, not into a library

The last step is where most digitization projects stop short. The standard is converted, uploaded and filed in an app, where it waits to be read. That is a [ghost SOP](/problems/ghost-sop) with better formatting.

Publishing should put the standard into today's work. In Mise, the standards owner publishes HSK-101 from the [standards workspace](/platform#standards) and assigns it to the room attendant role. The next time Maya Fernando opens the staff app on the demo property, Room 208 appears as her next timed task, with the countdown running and the steps on screen.

## The finished digital SOP

Here is the whole conversion side by side:

| | Paper binder | Timed task |
|---|---|---|
| Format | Paragraph of prose | 10 steps in 4 phases |
| When it is seen | During onboarding | Every time the room is done |
| Time expectation | None | 26 minutes, with a live countdown |
| What done looks like | Adjectives | 3 reference photos |
| Evidence | Supervisor walk-past | 2 photo gates, timestamps, sign-off |
| Version | Whatever is in the binder | Version recorded on every task |
| Output | Nothing | A line in the service record |

## A digital SOP template you can reuse

Use this structure for every standard you convert:

- **Code and name:** a short code and a plain title (HSK-101 Guest room reset and release).
- **Role:** who runs it.
- **Target time:** the defensible median, in minutes.
- **Phases and steps:** one action per step, grouped into Prepare, Perform, Verify, Release.
- **Reference photos:** what done looks like, for the steps where it matters.
- **Photo gates:** the two or three steps a guest or auditor would check.
- **Sign-off:** whether a supervisor must verify before release.
- **Version note:** what changed since the last version.

## Common mistakes when you convert paper SOPs to digital

1. **Copying the paragraph into the app.** If the step cannot be confirmed, it is not a step.
2. **Converting the whole library first.** Convert five to ten standards and run them. You will rewrite your first ones anyway.
3. **Aspirational target times.** They make every task look late and teach staff to ignore the timer.
4. **Photo-gating everything.** It slows the work and devalues the evidence.
5. **Publishing to a library.** A digital SOP nobody is assigned is still a document.

## Where to go next

If you are choosing where to start, most properties begin with housekeeping; our [housekeeping SOP app](/solutions/housekeeping) page shows how the rest of that department converts. When your first standards are running, the record they produce is the foundation of [hotel audit readiness](/audit-readiness).
